<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spoleto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Spoletium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Spoleto is a hill town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the Roman Spoletium. Taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> around 570, it became the seat of a powerful duchy that cut the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Ravenna, and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-iii-of-spoleto">Guy of Spoleto</a> was crowned emperor in 891. It later passed to the Papal States, and since 1958 it has hosted the Festival of the Two Worlds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spoleto.jpg" alt="Spoleto" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torsten Henning · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Spoleto_ty20060511r12040.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
